{"id": "De5v7R5Torc", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "Why do we say SWAHA at every havan? 🔥 Every time you say it, you’re calling a GODDESS. Did you know? Comment YES or NO 👇\n\nIn the Vedas, “svāhā” ends every offering into the fire: agnaye svāhā, “to Agni, svāhā”.\n\nBut the Devi Bhagavatam (Book 9, ch. 43) says Svaha is a NAME. Once, the offerings never reached the gods, and the gods went hungry. Brahma prayed, and from the Devi came a goddess: Svaha. She became Agni’s power to burn, and his wife. Brahma’s rule: end the offering with her name, and it reaches the gods. A mantra without Svaha, says the Purana, is like a snake without poison.\n\nAnd she isn’t alone:\n🪔 SVADHA, the name said when we offer to our ancestors (ch. 44)\n🪙 DAKSHINA, the gift to the priest (ch. 45)\nBoth are goddesses too.\n\nNext havan, tell your child whose name they’re saying. 🙏\n\n📚 Stories like this, for kids, in Rishi Katha’s picture books. 15K FAMILY DEAL: Books 1–3 for ₹350 (₹500), only till 25 Oct, midnight. Comment FAMILY and we’ll DM you the deal, or tap the link in bio → rishikatha.com\n\n💬 Save this for your next pooja, and share it with someone who says Swaha every day!\n\n#swaha #havan #hawan #yagna #homam agni hinduism sanatandharma devibhagavatam hindumythology indianculture pooja grihapravesh rishikatha kidsbooks", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1792899198, "like_count": 10482, "comment_count": 31, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1123037673987572a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 58.116, "asr": 44100, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.fblr22-1.fna.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m78/AQNXNy0pRda4_n3lkJ10DmCEHqA_YRPl816s2-dGXRfBPwqSLHvJpswFj6_baAUmNdA2Uf_qHEEGwLUGEYfIg85xfv6vkSrRC9qQyB0.mp4?_nc_cat=102&_nc_oc=AdoFk1ERavP3LXVXuMLK5ZJR0MRH8r525y7ogKDwaG9UuKsZrsGnHW0r__n